  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species occurs throughout most of Europe up to 64° N in Scandinavia. In Spain, it is present only in the northern half and the Sierra Nevada. It occurs from sea level to 700 m.<br/><br/>In the EU 27 region, the species' area of occupancy is 251,284 km² and the extent of occurrence is 7,583,725 km².<br/><br/>The global range of this species extends throughout the Palearctic. The global distribution area of the species is situated both within and outside Europe.",
    "population": "This is a common and widespread species in its European range. Population and distribution data show a small expansion of the species in the last decade.",
    "habitats": "In temperate Europe, the Peacock is one of the best-known butterflies, because of its striking appearance and common occurrence. There is no other diurnal butterfly with such noticeable eye spots on the upperside of its wings. In good years, the Peacock can be present in large numbers. It is often seen in gardens and parks on herbaceous borders and flowering shrubs, looking for nectar. Eggs are laid on Stinging Nettle (<em>Urtica dioica</em>) but occasionally Hop (<em>Humulus lupulus</em>) is also used. Large patches of foodpants are preferred, growing in full sun and in sheltered places such as the edges of woodland.&#160;The caterpillar is black with branched black spines and many tiny white spots. It looks similar to the caterpillar of the Map (<em>Araschnia levana</em>) but has prominent spines or thorns on its head. The caterpillars live gregariously in flimsy webs. Sometimes, plants are completely covered with spun silk, which serves as a home for tens of caterpillars. They leave the web to pupate either on the foodplant or in surrounding vegetation. The adult butterfly goes into hibernation, sometimes using cold lofts and sheds. There can be as many as three generations a year, depending on the geographical position and the weather conditions. Habitats include urban parks and large gardens (12%), towns, villages, industrial sites (11%), tree lines, hedges, small woods, bocage, parkland dehesa (9%), broad-leaved deciduous forests (8%), mixed woodland (7%), mesophile grasslands (6%), fallow land, waste places (5%) (van Swaay <em>et al.</em> 2006).",
    "threats": "This species is not believed to face major threats at the European level.",
    "measures": "This species occurs in a number of protected areas across its range. No specific conservation actions are needed at the European level, although populations should still be monitored by a Butterfly Monitoring Scheme.",
    "use_trade": "All butterflies are collected to some extent, but only for the extremely rare species can it be a problem and the trade in Europe is generally at a low level compared to other continents. There is no specific trade information for this species.",
    "rationale": "European regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/>EU 27 regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/><br/>Within the European region, the Peacock butterfly occurs thought most of Europe. This species is listed as Least Concern, as it has been evaluated against all the IUCN criteria and does not meet, nor is it close to meeting, the thresholds for any category of threat in Europe or the EU 27 region.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
